WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.560
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.160
Today I will try to show&nbsp;
you the incredible progress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.120
in computer graphics research through the&nbsp;
lens of bubbles in computer simulations.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:19.840
Yes, bubbles indeed. Approximately a year ago,&nbsp;
we covered a technique which could be used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:26.480
to add bubbles to an already existing fluid&nbsp;
simulation. This paper appeared in 2012 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:32.720
described a super simple method that helped us&nbsp;
compute where bubbles appear and disappear over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:39.120
time. The best part of this was that this could&nbsp;
be added after the simulation has been finalized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:44.960
which is an insane value proposition. If we&nbsp;
find ourselves yearning for some bubbles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:50.800
we just add it afterwards, if we don’t like the&nbsp;
results, we can take them out with one click.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.800
Now, simulations are not only about sights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:02.400
what about sounds? In 2016, this paper did&nbsp;
something that previously seemed impossible:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.520
it took this kind of simulation data,&nbsp;
and made sure that now, we can not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:25.840
add bubbles to a plain water simulation,&nbsp;
but also simulate how they would sound.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.480
On the geometry side, a followup paper appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:37.840
just a year later that could simulate a handful&nbsp;
of bubbles colliding, sticking together.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:44.720
Then, three years later, in 2020, Christopher&nbsp;
Batty’s group also proposed a method&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:51.040
that was capable of simulating merging and&nbsp;
coalescing behavior on larger-scale simulations.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:58.160
So, what about today’s paper? Are we going&nbsp;
even larger with hundreds of thousands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:04.720
or maybe even millions of bubbles? No,&nbsp;
we are going to take just one bubble…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:12.080
or at most a handful, and have a real close look&nbsp;
at a method that is capable of simulating these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.840
beautiful evolving rainbow patterns. The&nbsp;
key to this work is that it is modeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:23.760
how the thickness of the surfaces changes&nbsp;
over time. That makes all the difference.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:30.160
Let’s look under the hood and observe how much&nbsp;
of an effect the evolving layer thickness has on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:36.800
the outputs. The red color coding represents&nbsp;
thinner, and the blue shows us the thicker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:43.440
regions. This shows us that some regions in these&nbsp;
bubbles are more than twice as thick as others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:51.760
And there are also more extreme cases, there is&nbsp;
a six-time difference between this and this part.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.400
You can see how the difference in&nbsp;
thickness leads to waves of light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.920
interfering with the bubble&nbsp;
and creating these beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:07.040
rainbow patterns. You can’t get this without&nbsp;
a proper simulator like this one. Loving it.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:12.720
This variation in thicknesses is responsible&nbsp;
for a selection of premium-quality effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:19.640
in a simulation beyond surface vortices,&nbsp;
interference patterns can also be simulated, and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.360
deformation-dependent rupturing of soap films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.520
This incredible technique can&nbsp;
simulate all of these phenomena.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.720
And now, our big question is, okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:40.640
it simulates all these, but how well does it do&nbsp;
that? It is good enough to fool the human eye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:46.160
but how does it compare to the&nbsp;
strictest adversary of all… reality!

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:51.760
I hope you know what is coming. Oh yeah!&nbsp;
Hold on to your papers, because now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.560
we will let reality be our judge and&nbsp;
compare the simulated results to that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.520
That is one of the biggest challenges&nbsp;
in any kind of simulation research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:08.320
so, let’s see. This is a piece of real footage of&nbsp;
a curved soap film surface, where these rainbow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:15.360
patterns get convected by an external force field.&nbsp;
Beautiful. And now, let’s see the simulation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:24.640
Wow, this has to be really close. Let’s&nbsp;
see them side by side and decide together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:34.160
Whoa. The match in the swirly region here is just&nbsp;
exceptional. Now, note that even if the algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:40.720
is a 100% correct, this experiment cannot be a&nbsp;
perfect match because not only the physics of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:47.040
the soap film has to be simulated correctly, but&nbsp;
the forces that move the rainbow patterns as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:52.400
We don’t have this information from the&nbsp;
real-world footage, so the authors had to try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:59.600
reproduce these forces, which is not part of the&nbsp;
algorithm, but a property of the environment. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:06.480
I would say that this footage is as close as&nbsp;
one can possibly get. My goodness, well done!

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.040
So, how much do we have to pay for&nbsp;
this in terms of computation time?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:19.200
If you ask me, I would pay at the very least&nbsp;
double for this. And if you have been holding on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:23.360
to your papers so far, now, squeeze that paper,&nbsp;
because now comes the best part, because in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:29.280
cheaper cases, only 4 to 7% extra&nbsp;
computation, which is outrageous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:36.320
There is this more complex case, with the large&nbsp;
deforming sphere. In this case, the new technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:42.400
indeed makes a huge difference. So, how much&nbsp;
extra computation do we have to pay for this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:51.280
Only 31%. 31% extra computation&nbsp;
for this? That is a fantastic deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:57.040
you can sign me up right away. As you see, the&nbsp;
pace of progress in computer graphics research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:02.400
is absolutely incredible, and these simulations&nbsp;
are just getting better and better by the day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:09.680
Imagine what we will be able to do just two more&nbsp;
